messages.content:
You maintain a graph memory for an assistant. You read one note and answer with a memory patch:
one JSON object and nothing else, no prose, no code fence, matching this JSON schema:

{
 "type": "object",
 "additionalProperties": false,
 "properties": {
  "add_facts": {
   "type": "array",
   "description": "New durable facts the note states, one standalone sentence each.",
   "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "required": [
     "content",
     "entities"
    ],
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "properties": {
     "content": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The fact as a declarative sentence that names its subjects."
     },
     "entities": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
       "type": "string"
      },
      "description": "Every entity the fact is about, by name."
     },
     "kind": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "A short category: \"fact\", \"constraint\", \"decision\", \"preference\"."
     },
     "confidence": {
      "type": "number",
      "minimum": 0,
      "maximum": 1
     },
     "evidence": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The shortest quote from the note that states this."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  "corrections": {
   "type": "array",
   "description": "Existing facts the note changes. Use these instead of adding a contradicting fact.",
   "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "required": [
     "new_content"
    ],
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "properties": {
     "old_memory_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The id of the existing memory as given, a decimal string such as \"883768514279557120\". Ids are strings because JSON numbers lose precision above 2**53."
     },
     "old_text": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The existing fact's content, when its id is not known."
     },
     "new_content": {
      "type": "string"
     },
     "entities": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
       "type": "string"
      },
      "description": "Entities of the corrected fact; omit to keep the old ones."
     },
     "kind": {
      "type": "string"
     },
     "confidence": {
      "type": "number",
      "minimum": 0,
      "maximum": 1
     },
     "evidence": {
      "type": "string"
     }
    }
   }
  },
  "add_relations": {
   "type": "array",
   "description": "Relationships between two entities the note establishes.",
   "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "required": [
     "src",
     "dst",
     "rel_kind"
    ],
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "properties": {
     "src": {
      "type": "string"
     },
     "dst": {
      "type": "string"
     },
     "rel_kind": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "A short verb: leads, maintains, owns, depends_on, member_of."
     },
     "evidence": {
      "type": "string"
     }
    }
   }
  },
  "remove_relations": {
   "type": "array",
   "description": "Relationships the note says no longer hold.",
   "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "required": [
     "src",
     "dst"
    ],
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "properties": {
     "src": {
      "type": "string"
     },
     "dst": {
      "type": "string"
     },
     "rel_kind": {
      "type": "string"
     },
     "evidence": {
      "type": "string"
     }
    }
   }
  },
  "entity_aliases": {
   "type": "array",
   "description": "A name the note uses for an entity that already exists under another name.",
   "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "required": [
     "name",
     "canonical"
    ],
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "properties": {
     "name": {
      "type": "string"
     },
     "canonical": {
      "type": "string"
     },
     "evidence": {
      "type": "string"
     }
    }
   }
  }
 }
}

How to fill it in:
- A fact is one durable statement the note makes, written as a standalone declarative sentence
  that names its subjects, so it can be read on its own later. List every entity the fact is
  about (people, teams, systems, places, things) by name.
- Use the existing entity names given with the note whenever the note refers to the same
  thing. When the note uses a different name for an existing entity, add an entity_aliases
  entry mapping the note's name to the existing name.
- When the note changes something an existing fact states, do not add a contradicting fact.
  Add a correction that copies the existing fact's id into old_memory_id exactly as given (it
  is a string), gives the new content, and lists the entities of the corrected fact. Move the
  relationships that change: put the old one in remove_relations and the new one in
  add_relations.
- A relation joins two entities with a short rel_kind such as leads, maintains, owns,
  depends_on, member_of, reports_to. Add one for each relationship the note states.
- Quote the shortest span of the note that supports each entry in its evidence field.
- Do not invent anything the note does not say. Skip greetings, questions and speculation.
- If the note contains nothing durable, return an object whose arrays are empty.

message.reasoning:
The note says: "reporting needs the ledger service (the nightly balance export)."

So a new relation: reporting service depends_on ledger service. The note says "reporting" which refers to "reporting service" - an existing entity. Should I add an entity alias? The note uses "reporting" for "reporting service". Existing entity names include "reporting service". So I could add an alias "reporting" -> "reporting service".

Facts: is this durable? "reporting needs the ledger service for the nightly balance export" - yes, a dependency fact. I could add it as a fact and/or a relation. The schema says add_relations for relationships between two entities the note establishes. A fact could also be added: "The reporting service needs the ledger service for the nightly balance export." Hmm, but the primary mechanism for dependencies is add_relations with rel_kind depends_on. I think adding a relation is enough; possibly also a fact. The instructions: "A fact is one durable statement the note makes". The dependency is a durable statement. But guidance typically: relations cover dependencies. I'll add the relation and possibly a fact. To be safe, add both? Adding a fact duplicating a relation might be redundant. The examples suggest add_relations for relationships; facts for standalone statements. I'll include the relation with evidence "reporting needs the ledger service (the nightly balance export)" and an alias for "reporting" -> "reporting service".

Also, could add a fact about the nightly balance export? "The reporting service performs a nightly balance export" — that's inferring slightly. The note says reporting needs the ledger service (the nightly balance export) — meaning the ledger service provides the nightly balance export. That could be a fact: "The ledger service provides the nightly balance export." That's a reasonable durable fact from the note. But do not invent anything the note does not say. The parenthetical clarifies what the ledger is needed for: the nightly balance export. I could include it as part of the relation evidence only. I'll keep it simple: one relation, one alias.

Actually should I add a fact too? The relation captures the dependency. I'll add a fact as well? The schema description for relations: "Relationships between two entities the note establishes." The dependency is a relationship, so relation is appropriate. I'll not add a redundant fact.

Output JSON:
{
 "add_relations": [
  {
   "src": "reporting service",
   "dst": "ledger service",
   "rel_kind": "depends_on",
   "evidence": "reporting needs the ledger service (the nightly balance export)"
  }
 ],
 "entity_aliases": [
  {
   "name": "reporting",
   "canonical": "reporting service",
   "evidence": "reporting needs the ledger service"
  }
 ]
}

The user-level instruction says output valid JSON ending with code fence, but the system prompt says one JSON object and nothing else, no code fence. System prompt takes precedence: "one JSON object and nothing else, no prose, no code fence". The user message contradicts; system wins. Output raw JSON.

